Write the choice name, `::` and the variant name together, with no whitespace or comments around the separator. A unit variant
takes no parentheses. A variant with fields requires parentheses, including
when every field has a default.

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    WindowEvent = {
        Opened,
        Resized | width I32, height I32 = 720 |,
        Captioned | text String? = "Untitled" |,
    }

    opened = WindowEvent::Opened
    wide = WindowEvent::Resized(1280)
    tall = WindowEvent::Resized(1280, height = 1080)
    caption = WindowEvent::Captioned()
    blank = WindowEvent::Captioned(none)
]

`wide` carries width 1280 and height 720. `caption` carries the present
String `"Untitled"`. `blank` carries `none` because the caller supplied it.

### Argument rules

Positional arguments fill fields in declaration order. Named arguments can
follow in any order and may skip a defaulted field. Each field can receive one
argument. Unknown names, extra arguments and omitted required fields are
errors. An eligible raw numeric literal takes its exact field type. An
already typed argument must match that type.

`WindowEvent::Opened()` adds parentheses to a unit variant.
`WindowEvent::Captioned` omits parentheses from a payload variant.
Both forms are errors. Inspect a completed payload with a pattern.

### Constructor routes

An ordinary `WindowEvent` receiver constructs the value immediately. At a
`Route of WindowEvent` parameter, `WindowEvent::Resized(1280)` captures the
completed payload, including the default height. At a
`Route of I32 -> WindowEvent` parameter, the same spelling captures the width
and reserves the final height for future input, even though it has a default.
The captured arguments form a contiguous leading prefix and run once in
written order. See @../wiring (Wiring).

### MON choice data

Moth source uses a qualified name such as `WindowEvent::Opened`. In MON data,
`::Opened` reads the expected choice from the receiving schema. An explicit
qualifier must match that schema. MON applies validated field defaults to
omissions without evaluating Moth expressions. See @../mon/#mon-format (MON format).
